/// One secret belonging to one integration, encrypted at rest.
///
/// WHY NOT ON `StoreSetting`? Because `GET /settings/public` serves that row to
/// anyone, and it does so with `findUnique` + `include` rather than a whitelist
/// `select` — so every column on it is one careless edit away from being
/// published. That is the exact reason these keys originally lived in the server
/// environment, and moving them into the database does not repeal it.
///
/// A separate table cannot be leaked by a careless edit to a different query: an
/// endpoint that never joins to this model cannot serve it. That is a structural
/// guarantee rather than a "remember to exclude these fields" one, which is why
/// the storage moved but the prohibition did not. Do not add a credential column
/// to `StoreSetting`, and do not join this model into any public projection.
///
/// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// `value` IS CIPHERTEXT, NEVER PLAINTEXT.
///
/// AES-256-GCM under `INTEGRATION_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, written and read only through
/// `src/app/lib/crypto.ts`, stored as `v1:<iv>:<tag>:<ciphertext>`. Nothing may
/// write this column directly — a plaintext value written by hand is refused on
/// read rather than honoured, deliberately, so a shortcut fails loudly instead of
/// silently disabling the encryption for that row.
///
/// The value is WRITE-ONLY as far as the API is concerned. No endpoint returns
/// it: reads disclose only whether a credential is present, plus `lastFour`. A
/// value the API can return is a value an XSS or a logged response can capture.
///
/// One row per secret, rather than a column per credential, because integrations
/// need different numbers of them — Steadfast two, the Meta CAPI one, a future
/// courier four. A wide table would be mostly null and would need a migration
/// every time an integration was added. The cost is that the schema does not
/// enforce WHICH kinds an integration requires; that lives in the provider's
/// declared credential descriptor and in Zod, which is where this codebase
/// already enforces shapes the database cannot.
///
/// See openspec/changes/rename-courier-setting-to-integrations, design.md
/// Decision 1.
model IntegrationCredential {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  provider    String
  integration Integration @relation(fields: [provider], references: [provider], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Which secret this is, in the integration's own vocabulary: `apiKey`,
  /// `secretKey`, `webhookToken`, `accessToken`. The set of valid kinds for a
  /// given provider is declared by that provider's credential descriptor.
  kind String

  /// The encrypted secret. See the model comment — never written or read except
  /// through `crypto.ts`.
  value String

  /// The last four characters of the plaintext, for showing WHICH value is
  /// stored without disclosing it.
  ///
  /// The only thing derived from a credential that ever leaves the server. Four
  /// characters is useless to an attacker and sufficient for a merchant to tell
  /// "the key I just pasted" from "the one from last year", which is the entire
  /// question the admin panel needs answered. Short secrets are masked whole —
  /// four of six characters is not a hint.
  lastFour String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// One value per kind per integration. An upsert target: saving a credential
  /// REPLACES the previous one, and the old ciphertext is gone rather than
  /// accumulating as history — a superseded secret kept around is a secret that
  /// can still leak.
  @@unique([provider, kind])
}
